.TH NERVOUS 1
.SH NAME
nervous \- compile and run Nervous, a concurrent language and register virtual machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nervous
[
.B \-sG
]
[
.B \-H
.I heapwords
]
[
.B \-o
.I words
]
[
.B \-a
.I file
|
.B \-A
.I v2file
|
.B \-b
.I bytecode
|
.B \-c
.I source
|
.B \-f
.I file
|
.B \-F
.I v2file
|
.B \-r
.I source entry
[
.I args ...
]
|
.B \-x
.I bytecode entry
[
.I args ...
]
|
.B \-t
.I bytecode entry
[
.I args ...
]
|
.B \-X
.I bytecode entry
[
.I args ...
]
]
.PP
.B nervous
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nervous
is an experimental Erlang-like concurrent language and a portable
64-bit register virtual machine.  Source files conventionally end in
.BR .nv .
Exactly one mode flag selects what
.I nervous
does; with no mode flag and no arguments it prints its version string
and exits.  Every mode reads a single named file; only
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-t
and
.B \-X
also take a trailing
.I entry
function name and, after it, an argument list for that function
(see
.B "ARGUMENTS"
below). Passing trailing arguments to any other mode is a usage
error.
.PP
The common flags, meaningful whenever a program actually executes
(\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-x\fR, \fB\-t\fR, \fB\-X\fR), are:
.TP
.B \-s
After the run, print scheduler statistics to standard error: wall
time, processes spawned and peak live, dispatches and reductions,
messages sent and nanoseconds per message, collection counts, and
host allocation high-water bytes. The wall time is measured from
just before the scheduler (or, for
.BR \-x / \-t ,
the standalone executor) is created, so it excludes lexing, parsing
and compiling \(em only
.B \-r
and
.B \-X
build a scheduler, so
.B \-s
has no effect under
.BR \-b ,
.B \-x
or
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-G
Force a garbage collection at every allocating instruction's
reservation, instead of only when the process heap is under
pressure. Intended for exercising the collector in testing; slower
than the default. Equivalent to setting
.B $nervous_gcstress
to
.B 1
in the environment, which
.I nervous
also honors as the default when
.B \-G
is absent; an empty or
.B 0
value means normal execution, and any other value is a usage error
named
.BR environment .
.TP
.BI \-H " heapwords"
Set the per-process live-object word budget (used heap objects,
adopted fragments and retained frame-stack capacity).
.I heapwords
of
.B 0
(the default) means unlimited; collection still bounds garbage
regardless.  A process that cannot fit inside a non-zero budget after
collecting faults
.BR system_limit .
.TP
.BI \-o " words"
Set the off-process collection threshold (D074): a process whose heap
holds at least
.I words
used-plus-adopted words at collection time runs that collection in a
separate forked proc instead of inline, so a large collection pauses
only the process being collected, not the whole scheduler.
.I words
of
.B 0
(the default) means never off-process, matching every existing
benchmark and this flag's absence before it existed; a
.I words
of
.B 1
forces every real collection off-process, since every execution heap
holds at least one live word (its argument tuple).  Equivalent to
setting
.B $nervous_gcoffload
to
.I words
in the environment, which
.I nervous
also honors as the default when
.B \-o
is absent, the same way
.B $nervous_gcstress
supplies
.BR \-G 's
default; an empty value means never off-process and any other
malformed value is a usage error named
.BR environment .
Unlike
.B \-H
and
.BR \-G ,
this has no effect under
.B \-x
or
.B \-t
(the standalone single-process executor has no scheduler and
therefore no off-process collection mechanism at all); it applies
only under
.B \-r
and
.BR \-X .
.PP
The mode flags:
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Format
.I file
(current syntax) to standard output. Formatting is stdout-only and
preserves comment text and order, though a trailing comment may move
to a standalone line.
.TP
.BI \-F " v2file"
Like
.BR \-f ,
but read
.I v2file
in the previous (v2) surface syntax
.RI ( "fn name { ${..} => body; }" ,
call-shaped
.BR send / spawn )
and rewrite it into current syntax. Use this once to migrate old
source; do not depend on the v2 syntax otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-a " file"
Parse
.I file
(current syntax) and print its abstract syntax tree to standard
output. Diagnostic only.
.TP
.BI \-A " v2file"
Like
.BR \-a ,
but parse
.I v2file
in the v2 compatibility syntax.
.TP
.BI \-c " source"
Compile
.I source
to bytecode and print it, verified, in the "Symbolic Bytecode v0"
text format to standard output (see
.B "BYTECODE FILES"
below). This performs the same lexing, parsing, code generation and
verification
.B \-r
does, but stops before execution; save the output to a file to
separate compilation from a later run.
.TP
.BI \-b " bytecode"
Read
.I bytecode
(a file previously produced by
.BR \-c ),
verify it, and print its disassembly to standard output. Does not
execute anything; a bare sanity check of a saved artifact.
.TP
.BI \-r " source entry " "[ args ... ]"
Compile
.I source
and run it: spawn
.I entry
as the root process under the full cooperative scheduler, with
process spawning, message passing, timers and host
.BR print / eprint
output all available, and step the scheduler until the root process
and every process it can still affect have settled. Prints the
root's final value (or, on an uncaught exit, that exit reason) to
standard output.
.TP
.BI \-x " bytecode entry " "[ args ... ]"
Read and verify
.I bytecode
(as
.BR \-b ),
then run
.I entry
directly with no process scheduler and no host callback table
installed. This executes only straight-line, single-process
computation: any instruction that needs a host \(em
.BR self ,
.BR spawn ,
.BR send ,
.BR receive ,
.B print
or
.B eprint
\(em faults
.B bad_process_context
immediately. Use
.B \-X
instead for a program that uses processes.
.TP
.BI \-t " bytecode entry " "[ args ... ]"
Like
.BR \-x ,
but additionally trace every executed instruction (reduction count,
function, program counter and opcode) to standard error, and prefix
the final result with
.BR "value " .
Same single-process restriction as
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BI \-X " bytecode entry " "[ args ... ]"
Read and verify
.I bytecode
(as
.BR \-b ),
then run
.I entry
under the same full process scheduler
.B \-r
uses \(em the same host callbacks, I/O streams and
.B \-s
statistics \(em rather than the standalone executor
.B \-x / \-t
use. This is the pairing for
.BR \-c :
compile once, save the result, and run it as many times as needed
without repeating lexing, parsing or code generation, while still
supporting programs that spawn processes and pass messages (unlike
.BR \-x / \-t ).
.SH ARGUMENTS
For
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-x ,
.B \-t
and
.BR \-X ,
each word after
.I entry
becomes one element of the tuple passed as
.IR entry 's
single argument, in order.  A word beginning with a quote
.RI ( ' )
followed by at least one more character becomes the atom named by
the rest of the word (the same quoted-atom convention as source
literals); any other word must be a valid signed 64-bit decimal
integer (Nervous has no other literal argument forms), and is passed
as an integer term.  Anything else is a usage error named
.BR argument .
.SH "BYTECODE FILES"
The file
.B \-c
writes and
\fB\-b\fR/\fB\-x\fR/\fB\-t\fR/\fB\-X\fR
read is the "Symbolic Bytecode v0" module format documented in
.B docs/bytecode.md
in the Nervous source tree: a canonical, line-oriented, human-
readable text encoding (module header, constant pool, then one
function per
.B func
\&...
.B end
block, one instruction per line), not a packed binary format. It
exists as a stable fixture and diagnostic aid, and to let compilation
be timed and cached separately from execution; it is explicitly not
the language's final portable encoding.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On success,
.I nervous
exits with the empty status. On failure it exits with one of the
following short status strings, and a diagnostic on standard error:
.BR usage
(bad flags or arguments to the command itself),
.BR environment
(a malformed
.B $nervous_gcstress
or
.B $nervous_gcoffload
value),
.BR parse ,
.BR compile ,
.BR bytecode
(a malformed
\fB\-b\fR/\fB\-x\fR/\fB\-t\fR/\fB\-X\fR
file),
.BR verify
(the loaded bytecode failed verification),
.BR argument
(a malformed entry argument, or out of memory building it),
.BR fault
(an uncaught process fault under
.BR \-x / \-t ),
.BR run
(a scheduler or root-process failure under
.BR \-r / \-X ),
and
.BR deadlock
(every remaining process is blocked and none is runnable).
.SH EXAMPLES
Format and compile:
.IP
.EX
nervous -f examples/arithmetic.nv
nervous -c examples/arithmetic.nv
.EE
.PP
Run a process-based program from source, with statistics:
.IP
.EX
nervous -s -r examples/ring.nv main 1000 2000
.EE
.PP
Compile once, then run the saved bytecode repeatedly without paying
for lexing, parsing or code generation on each run:
.IP
.EX
nervous -c examples/ring.nv > ring.nvbc
nervous -s -X ring.nvbc main 1000 2000
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/nervous/main.c
.br
.B lib
.SH SEE ALSO
.B README.md
and
.B docs/bytecode.md ,
.B docs/semantics.md ,
.B docs/decisions.md
in the Nervous source tree.
.br
.IR mk (1),
.IR rc (1)
.SH BUGS
The
.B \-x
and
.B \-t
modes cannot run any program that spawns a process, sends or
receives a message, or calls
.B print
or
.BR eprint ;
use
.B \-X
for those.
